Chapter 5. Controller Architecture

5.1 Controller $C_0$: realistic multi-loop PI

Definition 5.1. The reactive controller $C_0$ is defined as a multi-loop PI:

u_t^{(\ell)} = K_p^{(\ell)} (y_t^{(\ell)} - y_{\mathrm{ref}}^{(\ell)}) + K_i^{(\ell)} \int_0^t (y_s^{(\ell)} - y_{\mathrm{ref}}^{(\ell)}) \, ds

for each loop $\ell \in \{\text{flow rate}, T_{\mathrm{in}}, \text{pump speed}, \text{valve position}\}$.

Property 5.1. $C_0$ is structurally blind to any anticipated information. Its reaction latency is bounded below by the thermal detection time $\tau_{\mathrm{sensor}} + \tau_{\mathrm{transport}}$.

5.2 Controller $C_1$: reactive workload-aware

Definition 5.2. The controller $C_1$ is:

u_t = f(T_d(t), \hat{w}_{t+\Delta t})

For example, a proportional feedforward:

u_t = K_p (T_d(t) - T_{\mathrm{ref}}) + K_f \hat{w}_{t+\Delta t}

Property 5.2. $C_1$ exploits load information without predictive optimization. It is intermediate between $C_0$ and $C_2$.

5.3 Controller $C_2$: deterministic MPC

Definition 5.3. The controller $C_2$ solves, at each instant $t$:

u^*_{t:t+H} = \arg\min_{u_{t:t+H}} \sum_{k=0}^{H} \left[ \alpha E_{\mathrm{cool}}(t+k) + \beta W_{\mathrm{net}}(t+k) \right]

subject to:

x_{t+k+1} = f(x_{t+k}, u_{t+k}, \hat{w}_{t+k}; \theta)

T_{d,i}(t+k) \leq T_{\max}, \quad \forall i, k

\Delta P(t+k) \leq \Delta P_{\max}

u_{\min} \leq u_{t+k} \leq u_{\max}, \quad \Delta u_{\min} \leq \Delta u_{t+k} \leq \Delta u_{\max}

Property 5.3. $C_2$ assumes a perfect predictor. It does not explicitly handle uncertainty $\mathcal{U}_W$.

5.4 Controller $C_3$: workload-aware robust MPC

Definition 5.4. The controller $C_3^W$ solves the min-max problem:

u^*_{t:t+H} = \arg\min_{u_{t:t+H}} \max_{w_{t:t+H} \in \mathcal{W}_t} \sum_{k=0}^{H} \left[ \alpha E_{\mathrm{cool}}(t+k) + \beta W_{\mathrm{net}}(t+k) \right]

subject to the same constraints, for all $w \in \mathcal{W}_t$.

Property 5.4 (Robustness scope). By construction, $C_3^W$ guarantees constraint satisfaction for all $w \in \mathcal{W}_t$. However, $C_3^W$ is robust only to workload uncertainty, not to parametric uncertainty $\mathcal{U}_\theta$, model error $\mathcal{U}_M$, or temporal drift $\mathcal{U}_t$.

Critical clarification. The notation must distinguish:

· $C_3^W$: robust to workload;
· $C_3^{W\theta}$: robust to workload + parameters;
· $C_3^{\mathrm{full}}$: robust to workload + model + drift.

We retain $C_3^W$ as the main object of study, but the notation acknowledges that:

\boxed{\text{controller robustness} \neq \text{system robustness}}

5.5 Controller $C_4$: adaptive robust MPC (genuine competitor)

Definition 5.5. The controller $C_4$ combines MPC with an extended state observer (ESO) and online Jacobian linearization:

u^*_{t:t+H} = \arg\min_{u_{t:t+H}} J(u, \hat{w}_{t:t+H}, \hat\theta_t)

with online update of $\hat\theta_t$ by the observer, and the nonlinear problem converted to a QP via online linearization.

Critical repositioning (retained and strengthened). Published ARMPC work treats plant-model mismatch with online linearization and a disturbance observer, achieving 88.4% compensation of model error and 5.58% energy reduction versus baseline, while being 47× faster than an MIQP benchmark. Therefore, $C_4$ cannot be presented as a marginal "secondary benchmark." It represents a genuine methodological competitor:

\boxed{\text{explicit robustness} \quad \text{vs} \quad \text{online adaptation/estimation}}

5.6 Controller $C_5$: contextual distributionally robust optimization (new in Version 2)

Definition 5.6. The controller $C_5$ solves the CDRO problem:

u^*_{t:t+H} = \arg\min_{u_{t:t+H}} \max_{\mathbb{P} \in \mathcal{P}(\rho(\zeta_t))} \mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{P}} \left[ \sum_{k=0}^{H} \left( \alpha E_{\mathrm{cool}} + \beta W_{\mathrm{net}} \right) \right]

where $\mathcal{P}(\rho(\zeta_t))$ is a Wasserstein ball whose radius depends on the real-time context $\zeta_t$.

Property 5.5. CDRO is reported to reduce the robustness cost premium by approximately 13.7 percentage points relative to Min-Max MPC while maintaining near-zero thermal violations under spikes. This is the strongest published baseline for the workload-uncertainty problem.

5.7 Controller $C_6$: hierarchical control with workload intent (new in Version 2)

Definition 5.7. The controller $C_6$ is a two-level architecture. The upper level (scheduler interface) computes a job-class thermal intent signal:

\iota_t = \Psi(\text{job class}, \text{queue state}, \text{arrival rate})

The lower level (cooling controller) uses $\iota_t$ as an additional feedforward input:

u^*_{t:t+H} = \arg\min_{u_{t:t+H}} J(u, \hat{w}_{t:t+H}, \iota_t)

Property 5.6. Reported results show that feeding job-class thermal signatures from the scheduler to the cooling controller reduces thermal violations by 56.4% and cumulative overshoot by 60.2% versus a PI loop. This confirms the value of hierarchical control with workload intent, and makes $C_6$ a relevant comparator for the co-primary question.

5.8 Controller summary

Controller Uncertainty handled Adaptation Hierarchical
$C_0$ None No No
$C_1$ Workload (reactive) No No
$C_2$ None (nominal forecast) No No
$C_3^W$ Workload (min-max) No No
$C_4$ Model mismatch Yes (ESO) No
$C_5$ Workload (contextual DRO) Yes (context) No
$C_6$ Workload intent No Yes

Critical design decision. The co-primary question is resolved by comparing:

· Explicit robustness family: $C_3^W$ vs $C_5$;
· Adaptation family: $C_4$ vs $C_6$;
· Cross-family: $C_3^W$ vs $C_4$.

The key methodological requirement is that all controllers share the same information budget. If $C_4$ uses online estimation, its excitation comes from closed-loop data, which changes the identifiability analysis of Chapter 3. We therefore impose:

T_{\mathrm{id}}^{\mathrm{offline}}(C_3) + T_{\mathrm{online}}(C_3) = T_{\mathrm{id}}^{\mathrm{offline}}(C_4) + T_{\mathrm{online}}(C_4)

and report the information cost of each (offline experiment time for $C_3$ versus online excitation burden for $C_4$).

