Chapter 7. Robust Feasibility

7.1 Feasible versus infeasible robustness

Definition 7.1. The robust problem is feasible if:

\exists u_{t:t+H} : \forall w \in \mathcal{W}_t, \ x_{t+k+1} = f(x_{t+k}, u_{t+k}, w_{t+k}; \theta) \text{ and constraints satisfied}

Otherwise, it is robust infeasible.

7.2 Vector margin formulation

Critical correction. The earlier scalar bound $T_{d,i}^{\mathrm{nominal}} + \delta(\sigma_W) \leq T_{\max}$ covers only one thermal constraint. The full problem contains pressure, actuator, and rate constraints, plus parametric uncertainty. The correct formulation is a vector margin:

g_j(x, u, w, \theta) \leq 0, \quad j = 1, \dots, J

Robust feasibility becomes:

\boxed{\exists u : \sup_{w \in \mathcal{W}_t, \theta \in \mathcal{U}_\theta, M \in \mathcal{U}_M} g_j(x, u, w, \theta, M) \leq 0, \quad \forall j}

7.3 Set-inclusion monotonicity

Proposition 7.1 (Monotonicity by set inclusion). If $\mathcal{W}_1 \subseteq \mathcal{W}_2$, then:

\mathcal{F}(\mathcal{W}_2) \subseteq \mathcal{F}(\mathcal{W}_1)

Similarly, if $\mathcal{U}_{\theta, 1} \subseteq \mathcal{U}_{\theta, 2}$, then under the same model and constraints:

\mathcal{F}(\mathcal{U}_{\theta, 2}) \subseteq \mathcal{F}(\mathcal{U}_{\theta, 1})

Proof. A larger uncertainty set requires the existence of a control satisfying more scenarios. Any control satisfying $\mathcal{W}_2$ also satisfies $\mathcal{W}_1$, but not vice versa. $\square$

7.4 Feasibility mapping

Theorem 7.1 (Monotonicity of feasibility). For a fixed system, robust feasibility is decreasing in $\sigma_W$ and in the size of $\mathcal{U}_\theta$, and increasing in the available safety margin.

Proof. By Proposition 7.1, enlarging $\mathcal{W}_t$ or $\mathcal{U}_\theta$ shrinks the feasibility set. The safety margin increases the feasible set by relaxing the effective constraints. $\square$

