Chapter 9. Falsifiability and Kill Criteria with Pre-Registered Thresholds

9.1 Structural kill criteria

Definition 9.1 (Kill F1 — Structural non-identifiability). If $\lambda_{\min}(\mathcal{I}(\theta)) \to 0$ for all admissible excitations in a discretized grid of $\mathcal{U}$, then the model is structurally non-identifiable. The thesis must reduce model dimension.

Pre-registered threshold (new in Version 2): $\lambda_{\min}(\mathcal{I}) < 10^{-6}$ in normalized log-parameter coordinates.

Definition 9.2 (Kill F2 — Instrumental non-identifiability). If the model would be identifiable with ideal sensors but is not identifiable with realistic $\sigma_{T_d}, \sigma_{T_{cp}}, \dots$, then either the model must be reduced or the instrumentation improved.

Pre-registered threshold: practical identifiability fails if the condition number $\kappa(\mathcal{I}) > 10^6$ under realistic noise.

Definition 9.3 (Kill F3 — Non-identifiability in the controller band). If the model is identifiable at high frequency but not in $[0, \omega_H]$ relevant for MPC, the model is not usable for control.

Pre-registered threshold: $\lambda_{\min}(\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{band}}) < 10^{-4}$ for $\omega_H = 2\pi / (10 \Delta t)$.

Definition 9.4 (Kill F4 — Dominant structural error). If, even with $\theta = \hat\theta$, $\| T_{\mathrm{model}} - T_{\mathrm{meas}} \|$ exceeds a critical threshold, the model is structurally inadequate.

Pre-registered threshold: RMSE $> 1.0$°C on held-out data. This is calibrated against published hybrid physics plus data-driven models achieving 0.41°C RMSE on production data centers.

Definition 9.5 (Kill F5 — Instability of $\Gamma_3$). If $\Gamma_3(\hat\theta) \neq \Gamma_3(\theta)$ for small admissible perturbations, any conclusion on the superiority of $C_3$ is fragile.

Pre-registered threshold: $d_H(\Gamma_3(\hat\theta), \Gamma_3(\theta)) > 0.1$ in normalized regime coordinates.

Definition 9.6 (Kill F6 — Hydraulic mismatch). If the identified model predicts $T_d$ correctly but fails to predict $\Delta P$ with error $> 20\%$, the model is inadequate for hydraulic control.

9.2 Control kill criteria

Definition 9.7 (Kill A — Nominal failure). If $\mathcal{R}_3^{\mathrm{nominal}} = \varnothing$, $C_3$ never provides enough benefit.

Definition 9.8 (Kill B — Instability under parametric uncertainty). If $\mathcal{R}_3^{\mathrm{uncertain}}$ becomes significantly different or vanishes under perturbations compatible with $\mathcal{U}_\theta$, the apparent benefit of $C_3$ depends too strongly on the model.

Pre-registered threshold: Kill B triggers if $|\mathcal{R}_3^{\mathrm{uncertain}}| / |\mathcal{R}_3^{\mathrm{nominal}}| < 0.5$.

Definition 9.9 (Kill C — Substitution by adaptation). If $C_4$ or $C_6$ achieves the same robustness gains as $C_3$ at much lower cost, then formal robustness is not the right answer.

Pre-registered threshold: Kill C triggers if $C_4$ Pareto-dominates $C_3$ on the primary axes for more than 70% of the sampled regime points.

9.3 Falsifiability theorem

Critical correction. The earlier formulation — "the thesis is falsifiable if and only if at least one kill criterion is satisfied" — is incorrect. A falsification criterion does not need to be satisfied in the current data to render a hypothesis falsifiable. It suffices that a possible observation could contradict it.

Theorem 9.1 (Falsifiability).

\boxed{\text{Falsifiable} \iff \exists O \in \mathcal{O} \text{ such that } H \text{ is incompatible with } O}

where $\mathcal{O}$ is the space of observable outcomes.

Proof. A hypothesis is falsifiable if there exists an observable outcome incompatible with it. The kill criteria F1–F6 and A–C define such observables a priori. Their satisfaction is not required for falsifiability; their definability is. $\square$

Corollary 9.1. The doctoral thesis must include an explicit analysis of each kill criterion in its experimental protocol, declaring in advance which observations would refute the central hypothesis.

