Chapter 10. Synthetic and Field-Data Experimental Protocol

10.1 Global sequence

Definition 10.1.

\boxed{\text{IDENTIFY} \to \text{FORECAST} \to \text{CONTROL} \to \text{STRESS} \to \text{MAP } \mathcal{R}_3 \text{ and } \Gamma_3}

10.2 Phase IDENTIFY

Objective. Obtain the triplet $(\hat\theta, \mathcal{U}_\theta, \text{sufficiency proof})$.

Critical addition (new in Version 2): real-data milestone. Version 1's protocol was synthetic-only. This creates a credibility gap: identifiability conclusions drawn from a model that is also the data-generating process are optimistic by construction, and kill criterion F4 cannot be tested synthetically in a meaningful way. Version 2 therefore mandates a real-data IDENTIFY milestone using at least one public dataset.

Candidate datasets (identified, not yet acquired):

· The Frontier supercomputer cooling dataset (2023), which provides real-time measurements every 10 minutes including coolant supply/return temperatures, coolant flow rates, and power;
· A Kaggle dataset on data center cold source control;
· GPU power traces at fine granularity from the MIT Supercloud.

Sub-phases.

1. F0: Choice of minimal model (two-node, then three-node).
2. F1: Logarithmic reparameterization $\phi_i = \ln \theta_i$.
3. F2: Comparison of candidate excitations (step, PRBS, chirp, E-optimal), with band determined by identified time constants and amplitude constrained by $\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}$.
4. F3: OED-based sensor configuration selection (A-optimal and E-optimal).
5. F4: Computation of the trajectory sensitivity matrix and its eigenvalues; comparison with static FIM.
6. F5: Estimation of $\Sigma_\theta$ and bootstrap validation.
7. F6: Out-of-sample validation with distinct workloads.
8. F7: Residual analysis to detect $\mathcal{U}_M \neq \varnothing$.
9. F8: (New) Real-data validation on at least one public dataset, with kill criteria F1–F4 tested non-vacuously.

10.3 Phase FORECAST

Objective. Build a workload predictor with quantified uncertainty.

Levels.

P_0: \text{oracle}, \quad P_1: \text{deterministic}, \quad P_2: \text{probabilistic (conformal)}, \quad P_3: \text{adversarial}

Output. $\left( \hat{w}_{t:t+H}, \Sigma_W(t) \right)$ or Mondrian conformal intervals $[\hat{w} - q_\alpha^{(r)}, \hat{w} + q_\alpha^{(r)}]$ for each level, with conditional coverage reported separately on spike segments.

10.4 Phase CONTROL

Objective. Implement $C_0, C_1, C_2, C_3^W, C_4, C_5, C_6$ on the identified plant.

Protocol. For each combination $(z, C_i)$, run the simulation with the same workload traces and compute $\mathbf{J}(C_i)$.

Information budget constraint. All controllers share the same total identification + adaptation time:

T_{\mathrm{id}}^{\mathrm{offline}}(C_i) + T_{\mathrm{online}}(C_i) = T_{\mathrm{budget}}, \quad \forall i

10.5 Phase STRESS

Objective. Explore the experimental space:

z = (\eta_W, \mathcal{U}_\theta, H, \Lambda, \rho, Y_{\mathrm{sensors}}) \in \mathcal{Z}

Revised design (2+2 stratified).

· Primary axes: $\eta_W \in [0, 0.4]$, and the geometry of $\mathcal{U}_\theta$ (eigen-spectrum of $\Sigma_\theta$);
· Secondary slices: $H \in [1, 60]$ s, $Y_{\mathrm{sensors}} \in \{Y_2, Y_3, Y^\star_{\mathrm{OED}}\}$;
· Covariate (measured): $\Lambda \in [0.01, 1]$;
· Blocking factor: $\rho$ with at most two topologies.

Design of experiments. A Latin Hypercube or Sobol design with 500–1000 points on the two primary axes, complemented by focused slices on the secondary axes, and sensitivity analysis via Sobol indices.

10.6 Phase MAP $\mathcal{R}_3$ and $\Gamma_3$

Objective. Construct numerically the sets $\mathcal{F}$ and $\mathcal{R}_3$, and the boundary $\Gamma_3$, and analyze their stability.

Algorithms.

· Grid traversal with stratified sampling on the 2+2 design.
· Pareto front estimation by non-dominated sorting.
· Multi-objective optimization (NSGA-II or MOEA/D) for direct Pareto front estimation.
· Hausdorff distance computation by sampling within a compact study region.
· Monte-Carlo estimation of probabilistic stability $S_{\Gamma_3}(z)$.
· Copula-based joint sampling of $(w, \theta)$ from $\mathcal{U}_{W\theta}(\vartheta)$.

10.7 Joint compute-cooling benchmark (new in Version 2)

Objective. Provide an upper bound on achievable savings. Joint optimization of GPU parallelism, frequency, and cooling knobs is reported to yield 35.48% savings versus 23.4% for flow-only optimization in a large-scale cooling plant study. This is the ultimate benchmark against which the cooling-only controllers are compared. The gap between $C_3$ and the joint optimum quantifies the value of cross-layer coordination.

