Contribution 14 (New in Version 2). Explicit modeling of the water-energy-carbon coupling, exposing $T_{\mathrm{supply}}$ as a first-class control variable and including virtual water intensity $\lambda_{\mathrm{water}}$ in the carbon metric.

11.3 Final contribution

Theorem 11.1 (Answer to the doctoral question). The question "In which regions does robustification pay?" admits an answer in the form of a region:

\mathcal{R}_3 = \left\{ z : C_3 \text{ is non-dominated with respect to } C_2, C_4, C_5, C_6 \text{ and } B_{\mathrm{op}}(z) > 0 \right\}

If $\mathcal{R}_3^{\mathrm{nominal}} \neq \varnothing$ and if $\Gamma_3$ is stable under $\mathcal{U}_\theta$, then workload-aware robustness is justified in that region. Otherwise, the conclusion is that formal robustness is not cost-effective for D2C, which is a valid scientific result.

