Chapter 12. Conclusion

12.1 Synthesis

We have constructed a complete theoretical framework for workload-aware robustness of D2C cooling of AI systems. The approach is structured in five phases: thermo-hydraulic identification, workload forecasting with uncertainty, comparative control $C_0$–$C_6$, multi-parametric stress testing, and mapping of feasibility and performance regions.

The central result is the characterization of $\mathcal{R}_3$ as the set of regimes where the workload-aware robust MPC is non-dominated, together with the analysis of its stability under parametric uncertainty, and the resolution of the co-primary question of explicit robustness versus online adaptation.

12.2 Scope

This theory does not claim to invent a new coolant, a new controller, or a new architecture. It proposes a decision methodology: how to determine, in a falsifiable manner, whether formal robustness is justified in a given context. The answer can be positive, negative, or conditional — in all cases, it is informative.

12.3 Validity conditions

The theory is valid only if:

1. The identified model satisfies kill criteria F1–F4 and F6;
2. The boundary $\Gamma_3$ is stable (in the sense of Chapter 8), or its instability is quantified;
3. Workload predictors achieve error levels compatible with the regimes where $\mathcal{R}_3$ is non-empty;
4. The real-data IDENTIFY milestone (Phase F8) is completed and F4 is tested non-vacuously.

If any of these conditions fails, the theory provides a precise diagnostic of the failure mode, which is itself a usable result.

12.4 Open question

The deepest question remains: is robustness a property of the controller, or a property of the pair (controller, plant identifiability)? Proposition 8.1 and the R1–R4 partition suggest the second option: a robust controller built on a poorly identifiable model is not operationally robust. Formal robustness thus becomes a notion relative to identification quality, not an intrinsic property of the controller.

12.5 Final reflection

The chain

\boxed{\text{identifiability} \rightarrow \mathcal{U}_\theta \rightarrow \mathcal{F} \rightarrow \mathcal{R}_3 \rightarrow \Gamma_3 \rightarrow \text{robustification decision}}

is the true scientific object. The controllers are no longer the main subject. They become the instruments allowing the measurement of this relation.

The most interesting question is no longer "robust MPC for D2C." It is:

\boxed{\text{Robustification under imperfect identifiability: does the value of robustness depend on the amount of information the experiment actually provides about the plant?}}

This is the question the thesis is designed to answer, with a protocol that can conclude positively, negatively, or conditionally, and which has not yet been executed. 🌹

