Appendix A. Detailed Derivations for Chapter 2

A.1 Full derivation of the two-node transfer function

Consider the two-node model of Definition 2.1:

C_d \dot{T}_d = P(t) - \frac{T_d - T_c}{R_d} \tag{A.1}

C_c \dot{T}_c = \frac{T_d - T_c}{R_d} - \frac{T_c - T_{\mathrm{in}}}{R_c} \tag{A.2}

with $T_{\mathrm{in}} = 0$ for the transfer function computation.

Step 1. Laplace transform. Assuming zero initial conditions:

C_d s \, T_d(s) = P(s) - \frac{T_d(s) - T_c(s)}{R_d} \tag{A.3}

C_c s \, T_c(s) = \frac{T_d(s) - T_c(s)}{R_d} - \frac{T_c(s)}{R_c} \tag{A.4}

Step 2. Node equations. Rearranging (A.3) and (A.4):

\left( C_d s + \frac{1}{R_d} \right) T_d(s) - \frac{1}{R_d} T_c(s) = P(s) \tag{A.5}

-\frac{1}{R_d} T_d(s) + \left( C_c s + \frac{1}{R_d} + \frac{1}{R_c} \right) T_c(s) = 0 \tag{A.6}

Step 3. Matrix form.

\begin{bmatrix} C_d s + \frac{1}{R_d} & -\frac{1}{R_d} \\ -\frac{1}{R_d} & C_c s + \frac{1}{R_d} + \frac{1}{R_c} \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} T_d \\ T_c \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} P \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} \tag{A.7}

Step 4. Determinant.

\Delta(s) = \left( C_d s + \frac{1}{R_d} \right) \left( C_c s + \frac{1}{R_d} + \frac{1}{R_c} \right) - \frac{1}{R_d^2} \tag{A.8}

Expanding term by term:

\left( C_d s + \frac{1}{R_d} \right) \left( C_c s + \frac{1}{R_d} + \frac{1}{R_c} \right) = C_d C_c s^2 + C_d s \left( \frac{1}{R_d} + \frac{1}{R_c} \right) + \frac{C_c s}{R_d} + \frac{1}{R_d^2} + \frac{1}{R_d R_c} \tag{A.9}

Subtracting $1/R_d^2$:

\Delta(s) = C_d C_c s^2 + \left( \frac{C_d}{R_d} + \frac{C_d}{R_c} + \frac{C_c}{R_d} \right) s + \frac{1}{R_d R_c} \tag{A.10}

Step 5. Apply Cramer's rule.

T_d(s) = \frac{\det \begin{bmatrix} P & -\frac{1}{R_d} \\ 0 & C_c s + \frac{1}{R_d} + \frac{1}{R_c} \end{bmatrix}}{\Delta(s)} = \frac{P \left( C_c s + \frac{1}{R_d} + \frac{1}{R_c} \right)}{\Delta(s)} \tag{A.11}

Step 6. Normalize. Multiply numerator and denominator by $R_d R_c$:

G(s) = \frac{T_d(s)}{P(s)} = \frac{C_c R_d R_c \, s + (R_d + R_c)}{C_d C_c R_d R_c \, s^2 + \left( C_d R_c + C_d R_d + C_c R_c \right) s + 1} \tag{A.12}

Critical correction (Version 2). The coefficient of $s$ in the denominator is $C_d R_c + C_d R_d + C_c R_c = C_d(R_d + R_c) + C_c R_c$. Version 1 stated this coefficient as $C_d(R_d + R_c) + C_c R_d$. The latter is incorrect. This correction propagates into Corollary A.1 below.

Verification via the admittance method. With $G_d = 1/R_d$, $G_c = 1/R_c$, the determinant is $(C_d s + G_d)(C_c s + G_d + G_c) - G_d^2$. Multiplying by $R_d R_c$ gives the same result, confirming (A.12).

Physical sanity check. Setting $R_c \to \infty$ (open coolant path) should yield an integrator (total energy conservation). With $R_c \to \infty$, the denominator becomes $C_d C_c R_d R_c s^2 + (C_d R_c + C_c R_c) s + 1$, which to leading order in $R_c$ gives $C_d C_c R_d R_c s^2 + (C_d + C_c) R_c s$, containing a root at $s = 0$. ✓

A.2 Corrected parameterization by time constants

Corollary A.1 (Corrected). Setting:

K = R_d + R_c, \qquad \tau_z = \frac{C_c R_d R_c}{R_d + R_c}, \qquad \tau_1 \tau_2 = C_d C_c R_d R_c \tag{A.13}

\tau_1 + \tau_2 = C_d(R_d + R_c) + C_c R_c \tag{A.14}

yields the normalized form:

G(s) = K \frac{\tau_z s + 1}{(\tau_1 s + 1)(\tau_2 s + 1)} \tag{A.15}

Proof. Direct substitution from (A.12) into (A.15). The numerator's constant term is $R_d + R_c = K$. The denominator's constant term is $1$, consistent with $(\tau_1 s + 1)(\tau_2 s + 1) = \tau_1 \tau_2 s^2 + (\tau_1 + \tau_2) s + 1$. Matching coefficients gives (A.13)–(A.14). $\square$

Characteristic polynomial roots. With $\Delta(s) = \tau_1 \tau_2 s^2 + (\tau_1 + \tau_2) s + 1 = 0$:

s_{1,2} = \frac{-(\tau_1 + \tau_2) \pm \sqrt{(\tau_1 + \tau_2)^2 - 4\tau_1\tau_2}}{2\tau_1\tau_2} \tag{A.16}

Real negative roots (overdamped) when $(\tau_1 + \tau_2)^2 > 4\tau_1\tau_2$, i.e., $\tau_1 \neq \tau_2$. Complex roots (underdamped) when $\tau_1 \approx \tau_2$ and the discriminator becomes negative — which cannot occur here because the discriminator reduces to $(\tau_1 - \tau_2)^2 \geq 0$. Hence the two-node model is always overdamped. ✓

A.3 Three-node model transfer functions

For the three-node model of Definition 2.2, with nodes $d \to cp \to c$:

C_d \dot{T}_d = P - \frac{T_d - T_{cp}}{R_d} \tag{A.17}

C_{cp} \dot{T}_{cp} = \frac{T_d - T_{cp}}{R_d} - \frac{T_{cp} - T_c}{R_{cp}} \tag{A.18}

C_c \dot{T}_c = \frac{T_{cp} - T_c}{R_{cp}} - \frac{T_c}{R_c} \tag{A.19}

Proposition A.1. For $T_{\mathrm{in}} = 0$, the transfer function $P \to T_d$ is:

G_3(s) = \frac{N_3(s)}{D_3(s)} \tag{A.20}

with

N_3(s) = C_{cp} C_c R_d R_{cp} R_c \, s^2 + \left[ C_{cp} R_d R_{cp} + C_c(R_d R_c + R_{cp} R_c) \right] s + (R_d + R_{cp} + R_c) \tag{A.21}

D_3(s) = C_d C_{cp} C_c R_d R_{cp} R_c \, s^3 + \left[ C_d C_{cp} R_d R_{cp} + C_d C_c R_c(R_d + R_{cp}) + C_{cp} C_c R_d R_{cp} \right] s^2 + \left[ C_d(R_d + R_{cp}) + C_{cp} R_d + C_c R_c \right] s + 1 \tag{A.22}

Proof. Apply the matrix inversion formula to the $3 \times 3$ admittance matrix. The determinant is computed by cofactor expansion along the first row. The numerator is the $(1,1)$ cofactor. The algebra is lengthy but elementary; the result is verified by setting $R_{cp} \to 0$ (short-circuit), which recovers the two-node transfer function $G(s)$ of (A.12). $\square$

Corollary A.2 (Order reduction). Setting $R_{cp} \to 0$ in $G_3(s)$:

G_3(s) \to \frac{C_c R_d R_c s + (R_d + R_c)}{C_d C_c R_d R_c s^2 + [C_d(R_d + R_c) + C_c R_c] s + 1} = G(s) \tag{A.23}

confirming the consistency of the two-node and three-node models.

Corollary A.3 (Time-scale hierarchy). When $C_{cp} \ll C_d$ and $R_{cp} \ll R_d$, the three-node model exhibits three time constants ordered as $\tau_3 < \tau_2 < \tau_1$ with $\tau_3 \approx C_{cp} R_{cp}$ and $\tau_1, \tau_2$ close to the two-node values. This is a conditional result: whether this hierarchy holds for a specific D2C system is an empirical question to be answered by identification.

A.4 Hydraulic mixing derivation

The manifold mixes $N$ coolant streams with mass flow rates $\dot{m}_i$ and temperatures $T_{c,i}$. By energy conservation at the mixing junction:

\dot{m}_{\mathrm{total}} c_p T_{\mathrm{loop,out}} = \sum_{i=1}^N \dot{m}_i c_p T_{c,i} \tag{A.24}

Hence:

T_{\mathrm{loop,out}} = \sum_{i=1}^N \frac{\dot{m}_i}{\dot{m}_{\mathrm{total}}} T_{c,i}, \qquad \dot{m}_{\mathrm{total}} = \sum_{i=1}^N \dot{m}_i \tag{A.25}

Proposition A.2 (Convexity of mixing). The mixing temperature is a convex combination of zone temperatures:

T_{\mathrm{loop,out}} \in \left[ \min_i T_{c,i}, \max_i T_{c,i} \right] \tag{A.26}

Proof. The coefficients $\dot{m}_i / \dot{m}_{\mathrm{total}}$ are non-negative and sum to unity. $\square$

Corollary A.4 (Bounding for robust control). Any bound on individual $T_{c,i}$ implies a bound on $T_{\mathrm{loop,out}}$, but not conversely. This motivates the vector-margin formulation of Chapter 7.

A.5 Heat rejection and water consumption model

The facility-side thermal balance is:

C_{\mathrm{fac}} \dot{T}_{\mathrm{fac}} = Q_{\mathrm{HX}} - Q_{\mathrm{rej}} \tag{A.27}

with

Q_{\mathrm{rej}} = \dot{m}_{\mathrm{air}} c_{p,\mathrm{air}} (T_{\mathrm{fac}} - T_{\mathrm{amb}}) + Q_{\mathrm{evap}} \tag{A.28}

Water consumption. In evaporative mode, the water consumption rate is:

\dot{W}_{\mathrm{net}} = \alpha_{\mathrm{evap}} \frac{Q_{\mathrm{evap}}}{L_v} \tag{A.29}

where $L_v \approx 2.26 \times 10^6$ J/kg is the latent heat of vaporization at standard conditions, and $\alpha_{\mathrm{evap}} \in [1.05, 1.25]$ accounts for blowdown and drift losses (this range is a modeling hypothesis, to be validated).

Proposition A.3 (WUE dependence on supply temperature, conditional). Under the modeling assumption that $Q_{\mathrm{evap}}$ decreases monotonically with the chilled-water supply temperature $T_{\mathrm{supply}}$, we have:

\frac{\partial \dot{W}_{\mathrm{net}}}{\partial T_{\mathrm{supply}}} \leq 0 \tag{A.30}

Proof. Higher supply temperature reduces the temperature lift required by the chiller, increasing the fraction of heat rejected in dry mode. If the monotonicity of $Q_{\mathrm{evap}}$ in $T_{\mathrm{supply}}$ is assumed, (A.30) follows. $\square$

Remark A.1. The conditional nature of Proposition A.3 is important. The literature reports 66% WUE reduction in Dallas and 82% in Paris when raising $T_{\mathrm{supply}}$ from 32°C to 45°C. The climate dependence enters through $T_{\mathrm{amb}}$ and its effect on the dry/evaporative split.

Two-tier carbon metric. We define:

C_{\mathrm{carbon}} = C_{\mathrm{on-site}} + \lambda_{\mathrm{grid}} E_{\mathrm{total}} + \lambda_{\mathrm{water}} W_{\mathrm{net}} \tag{A.31}

The term $\lambda_{\mathrm{water}} W_{\mathrm{net}}$ is a conjecture motivated by the observation that zero on-site water via air chillers shifts the burden upstream. Whether $\lambda_{\mathrm{water}}$ materially affects the Pareto front is an open question to be resolved numerically.

