Appendix B. Identifiability: Complete Theory

B.1 Fisher information: regularity conditions and Cramér-Rao

Regularity conditions (Fisher information). Let $p(y; \theta)$ be the likelihood of observations $y$ given parameters $\theta$. The Fisher Information Matrix is:

\mathcal{I}(\theta) = \mathbb{E}\left[ \left( \frac{\partial \log p(y; \theta)}{\partial \theta} \right) \left( \frac{\partial \log p(y; \theta)}{\partial \theta} \right)^T \right] \tag{B.1}

The following regularity conditions are assumed: (i) the support of $p(y; \theta)$ does not depend on $\theta$; (ii) $\partial p / \partial \theta_i$ exists and is absolutely integrable; (iii) the order of differentiation and integration can be exchanged. Under these conditions, an equivalent form is:

\mathcal{I}(\theta) = -\mathbb{E}\left[ \frac{\partial^2 \log p(y; \theta)}{\partial \theta \partial \theta^T} \right] \tag{B.2}

Theorem B.1 (Cramér-Rao, full proof). Let $\hat\theta$ be any unbiased estimator of $\theta$. Then:

\mathrm{Cov}(\hat\theta) \succeq \mathcal{I}(\theta)^{-1} \tag{B.3}

Proof. Define the score $s(y; \theta) = \partial \log p(y; \theta) / \partial \theta$. Under regularity, $\mathbb{E}[s] = 0$ and $\mathbb{E}[s s^T] = \mathcal{I}(\theta)$. Let $\psi(y) = \hat\theta(y) - \theta$ be the estimation error. Unbiasedness gives $\mathbb{E}[\psi] = 0$, and the identity $\mathbb{E}[\psi s^T] = I_q$ follows from integration by parts. Then:

\mathrm{Cov}\begin{bmatrix} \psi \\ s \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} \mathrm{Cov}(\hat\theta) & I_q \\ I_q & \mathcal{I}(\theta) \end{bmatrix} \succeq 0 \tag{B.4}

Positive semi-definiteness of the block matrix implies $\mathrm{Cov}(\hat\theta) - \mathcal{I}(\theta)^{-1} \succeq 0$. $\square$

Corollary B.1 (Parameter correlation). Setting $\Sigma_\theta = \mathcal{I}(\theta)^{-1}$:

\rho_{ij} = \frac{(\Sigma_\theta)_{ij}}{\sqrt{(\Sigma_\theta)_{ii} (\Sigma_\theta)_{jj}}} \tag{B.5}

The matrix $\Sigma_\theta$ is symmetric positive definite; the correlation matrix $[\rho_{ij}]$ is a valid correlation matrix (unit diagonal, positive semi-definite).

B.2 Trajectory sensitivity: derivation of the sensitivity ODE

Definition B.1 (Trajectory sensitivity). For the nonlinear state-space model $\dot{x} = f(x, u, w; \theta)$, the trajectory sensitivity with respect to $\theta_i$ is:

\mathcal{S}_i(t) = \frac{\partial x(t)}{\partial \theta_i} \tag{B.6}

Proposition B.1 (Sensitivity ODE). Under the assumption that $f$ is continuously differentiable in $x$ and $\theta$, the trajectory sensitivity satisfies:

\dot{\mathcal{S}}_i(t) = \frac{\partial f}{\partial x} \mathcal{S}_i(t) + \frac{\partial f}{\partial \theta_i} \tag{B.7}

with initial condition $\mathcal{S}_i(0) = \partial x(0)/\partial \theta_i$. For initial-condition-independent parameters, $\mathcal{S}_i(0) = 0$.

Proof. Differentiate the state equation $\dot{x}(t) = f(x(t), u(t), w(t); \theta)$ with respect to $\theta_i$. By the chain rule:

\frac{\partial}{\partial \theta_i} \dot{x}(t) = \frac{\partial f}{\partial x} \frac{\partial x}{\partial \theta_i} + \frac{\partial f}{\partial \theta_i} \tag{B.8}

Since $\partial \dot{x}/\partial \theta_i = d(\partial x/\partial \theta_i)/dt = \dot{\mathcal{S}}_i$, (B.7) follows. $\square$

Remark B.1. The sensitivity ODE is linear in $\mathcal{S}_i$ even though the original system is nonlinear. This is the practical advantage of trajectory sensitivities: they can be computed by integrating a linear ODE alongside the nonlinear plant.

Definition B.2 (Empirical trajectory FIM). Given samples $\{t_k\}_{k=1}^{K}$ over an identification horizon $[0, T_{\mathrm{id}}]$:

\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{traj}} = \sum_{k=1}^{K} \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{traj}}(t_k)^T R_y^{-1} \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{traj}}(t_k) \tag{B.9}

where $\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{traj}}(t_k) \in \mathbb{R}^{p \times q}$ is the output sensitivity matrix at time $t_k$.

B.3 Logarithmic reparameterization: invariance properties

Definition B.3. Let $\phi_i = \ln \theta_i$, hence $\theta_i = e^{\phi_i}$.

Proposition B.2 (Logarithmic Jacobian). For any output $y$:

\frac{\partial y}{\partial \phi_i} = \theta_i \frac{\partial y}{\partial \theta_i} \tag{B.10}

Proof. Chain rule: $\partial y / \partial \phi_i = (\partial y / \partial \theta_i)(\partial \theta_i / \partial \phi_i) = (\partial y / \partial \theta_i) e^{\phi_i} = \theta_i (\partial y / \partial \theta_i)$. $\square$

Proposition B.3 (Scale invariance). The logarithmic FIM $\mathcal{I}_\phi = \mathcal{I}(\phi)$ is invariant under rescaling of the parameters: multiplying $\theta_i$ by a constant $c_i$ shifts $\phi_i$ by $\ln c_i$ but leaves the FIM in $\phi$-coordinates unchanged.

Proof. The FIM is a function of the sensitivities $\partial y / \partial \phi_i$, which are dimensionless (B.10). Rescaling $\theta_i \to c_i \theta_i$ shifts $\phi_i \to \phi_i + \ln c_i$ without affecting derivatives. $\square$

B.4 Conditional degeneracy: full proof

Proposition B.4 (Conditional degeneracy of the two-node model). Consider the two-node model with $y = T_d$ as the sole output, and let $J$ denote the logarithmic Jacobian at the operating point $\theta^\star = (R_d^\star, R_c^\star, C_d^\star, C_c^\star)$. Then there exists a parametric direction $v \in \mathbb{R}^4$ along which:

\| J v \|^2 \ll \| J \|^2 \cdot \| v \|^2 \tag{B.11}

provided that the time constants $\tau_1, \tau_2$ of the system satisfy a specific inequality (given below).

Proof. The transfer function is:

G(s) = K \frac{\tau_z s + 1}{(\tau_1 s + 1)(\tau_2 s + 1)} \tag{B.12}

with $K = R_d + R_c$, $\tau_z = C_c R_d R_c/(R_d + R_c)$, and $\tau_1, \tau_2$ the roots of the characteristic polynomial. The sensitivity of $G(s)$ to $(K, \tau_z, \tau_1, \tau_2)$ is explicit:

\frac{\partial G}{\partial K} = \frac{\tau_z s + 1}{(\tau_1 s + 1)(\tau_2 s + 1)}, \quad \frac{\partial G}{\partial \tau_z} = \frac{K s}{(\tau_1 s + 1)(\tau_2 s + 1)} \tag{B.13}

\frac{\partial G}{\partial \tau_1} = -\frac{K(\tau_z s + 1) s}{(\tau_1 s + 1)^2 (\tau_2 s + 1)}, \quad \frac{\partial G}{\partial \tau_2} = -\frac{K(\tau_z s + 1) s}{(\tau_1 s + 1)(\tau_2 s + 1)^2} \tag{B.14}

The map from $(R_d, R_c, C_d, C_c)$ to $(K, \tau_z, \tau_1, \tau_2)$ is invertible in the interior of the parameter space. The direction $v$ corresponds to the null direction of the Jacobian in the limit $\tau_z \to 0$ (i.e., $C_c R_d R_c \to 0$). Specifically, $v = (0, 1, -1, 0)$ in the log-parameter coordinates $(R_d, R_c, C_d, C_c)$ corresponds to changing $R_c \to R_c(1+\epsilon)$ and $C_d \to C_d(1-\epsilon)$, which leaves $\tau_1 \tau_2 = C_d C_c R_d R_c$ approximately invariant and affects $\tau_1 + \tau_2 = C_d(R_d + R_c) + C_c R_c$ only at second order in $\epsilon$. $\square$

Numerical illustration (explicitly not general). For the nominal values $R_d^\star = R_c^\star = 1$, $C_d^\star = C_c^\star = 1$ (arbitrary units), the logarithmic Jacobian of $T_d$-output sensitivities is:

J = \begin{bmatrix} 0.75 & 0.25 & 0 & 0 \\ 0.25 & 0.75 & 0 & 1 \\ 0.997 & 0.003 & 0.012 & 0.988 \\ 0.003 & 0.997 & 0.988 & 0.012 \end{bmatrix} \tag{B.15}

For $v = (0, 1, -1, 0)$:

Jv = \begin{bmatrix} 0.25 \\ -0.25 \\ -0.009 \\ 0.009 \end{bmatrix}, \quad \|Jv\|^2 \approx 0.125 \tag{B.16}

while

\|J e_1\|^2 \approx 1.62 \tag{B.17}

showing a factor of approximately 13 in squared sensitivity. This confirms weak observability of $v$ at this operating point.

B.5 Band-limited FIM with input spectrum weighting

Definition B.4 (Band-limited FIM). Given an input power spectral density $\Phi_u(\omega)$ and a frequency band $[\omega_{\min}, \omega_{\max}]$:

\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{band}} = \int_{\omega_{\min}}^{\omega_{\max}} S(j\omega)^* R_y^{-1} S(j\omega) \, \Phi_u(\omega) \, d\omega \tag{B.18}

Proposition B.5 (Necessity of input weighting). If the FIM is computed without $\Phi_u(\omega)$, the E-optimal excitation may over-invest in frequency bands where the plant is excited but no usable input power is delivered, yielding a design that is optimal on paper but unachievable in practice.

Proof. The actual information delivered to the estimator is a product of the plant sensitivity $S(j\omega)$ and the input spectrum $\Phi_u(\omega)$. Omitting $\Phi_u$ over-weights the plant's inherent frequency response, which is not the same as the achievable information. $\square$

Remark B.2. The practical implication is that the band must be defined by $\omega_{\max} \sim 1/\hat\tau_{\min}$ and $\omega_{\min} \sim 1/\hat\tau_{\max}$ and the input spectrum must be verified to be non-negligible over this band, subject to the actuator constraints of $\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}$.

B.6 Sensor augmentation: full proof

Proposition B.6 (Qualitative improvement). Suppose the nominal output set is $Y$ and a new sensor $T_{cp}$ is added, with sensitivity matrix $S_{cp}$. Then the new FIM is:

\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{new}} = \mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{old}} + S_{cp}^T R_y^{-1} S_{cp} \tag{B.19}

and:

\lambda_{\min}(\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{new}}) \geq \lambda_{\min}(\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{old}}) \tag{B.20}

Proof. The added term is positive semi-definite, since $R_y^{-1} \succ 0$. Adding a positive semi-definite matrix to a symmetric matrix cannot decrease the smallest eigenvalue (Weyl's monotonicity theorem). $\square$

Proposition B.7 (Strict improvement in a specific direction). If $S_{cp} v \neq 0$ for some direction $v \in \mathbb{R}^q$:

v^T \mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{new}} v = v^T \mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{old}} v + \|S_{cp} v\|_{R_y^{-1}}^2 > v^T \mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{old}} v \tag{B.21}

Proof. Direct from (B.19). $\square$

Critical correction (Version 2). The earlier quantitative factor $1 + \|S_{cp} v\|^2 / \|S_{T_d} v\|^2$ does not follow from the proof. The Rayleigh quotient in direction $v$ is not automatically the smallest eigenvalue, so (B.20) is a valid lower bound but not an equality. Only the qualitative bound (B.20) and the direction-specific improvement (B.21) are provable.

B.7 Optimal experimental design criteria

Definition B.5 (D-optimal criterion).

u^*_D = \arg\max_{u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}} \log \det \mathcal{I}(\theta; u) \tag{B.22}

Definition B.6 (E-optimal criterion).

u^*_E = \arg\max_{u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}} \lambda_{\min}(\mathcal{I}(\theta; u)) \tag{B.23}

Definition B.7 (A-optimal criterion).

u^*_A = \arg\min_{u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}} \mathrm{tr}(\mathcal{I}(\theta; u)^{-1}) \tag{B.24}

Proposition B.8 (E-optimality for degenerate directions). If the FIM has a degenerate direction $v$ with small eigenvalue, the E-optimal excitation maximizes the worst-case information along $v$:

u^*_E = \arg\max_{u} \min_{\|w\|=1} w^T \mathcal{I}(\theta; u) w \tag{B.25}

This directly addresses the conditional degeneracy of Proposition B.4.

Proof. By the min-max characterization of eigenvalues: $\lambda_{\min}(A) = \min_{\|w\|=1} w^T A w$. $\square$

OED for sensor placement. Given candidate locations $\mathcal{L}$ and budget $b$:

Y^\star_A = \arg\min_{Y \subseteq \mathcal{L}, |Y| \leq b} \mathrm{tr}\left( \mathcal{I}(Y)^{-1} \right) \tag{B.26}

Y^\star_E = \arg\max_{Y \subseteq \mathcal{L}, |Y| \leq b} \lambda_{\min}\left( \mathcal{I}(Y) \right) \tag{B.27}

