Appendix C. Uncertainty Sets and Conformal Prediction

C.1 Chi-square uncertainty set: derivation

Proposition C.1 (Asymptotic distribution of the ML estimator). Under regularity conditions, the maximum likelihood estimator $\hat\theta_{\mathrm{ML}}$ satisfies:

\sqrt{n} (\hat\theta_{\mathrm{ML}} - \theta^\star) \xrightarrow{d} \mathcal{N}(0, \mathcal{I}(\theta^\star)^{-1}) \tag{C.1}

where $n$ is the sample size.

Proof. Standard result; see Cramér-Rao and asymptotic normality of M-estimators. $\square$

Corollary C.1 (Chi-square set). Asymptotically:

(\hat\theta - \theta^\star)^T \mathcal{I}(\hat\theta) (\hat\theta - \theta^\star) \xrightarrow{d} \chi^2_q \tag{C.2}

Hence the $(1-\alpha)$ confidence set is:

\mathcal{U}_\theta^{\mathrm{stat}} = \left\{ \theta : (\theta - \hat\theta)^T \Sigma_\theta^{-1} (\theta - \hat\theta) \leq \chi^2_{q, 1-\alpha} \right\}, \quad \Sigma_\theta = \mathcal{I}(\hat\theta)^{-1} \tag{C.3}

Limitation. This set is asymptotic and requires the model to be correctly specified. For finite samples and model misspecification, the coverage may be inaccurate. This motivates the conformal alternative of Section C.2.

C.2 Conformal prediction: exchangeability and coverage

Definition C.1 (Exchangeability). A sequence $Z_1, \dots, Z_n$ is exchangeable if its joint distribution is invariant under permutation.

Theorem C.1 (Split conformal coverage). Let $\{(X_i, Y_i)\}_{i=1}^{n}$ be exchangeable. Split into a training set $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{train}}$ and a calibration set $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{cal}}$ of size $m$. Define the nonconformity score $s(x, y)$ and the empirical quantile:

q_{1-\alpha} = \mathrm{Quantile}_{1-\alpha}\left( \{ s(X_i, Y_i) : i \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{cal}} \} \cup \{+\infty\} \right) \tag{C.4}

Then the prediction set $\mathcal{C}(X_{n+1}) = \{ y : s(X_{n+1}, y) \leq q_{1-\alpha} \}$ satisfies:

\Pr\left[ Y_{n+1} \in \mathcal{C}(X_{n+1}) \right] \geq 1 - \alpha \tag{C.5}

Proof. By exchangeability, $s(X_{n+1}, Y_{n+1})$ is exchangeable with the calibration scores. The rank of the test score among the $m+1$ scores is uniform on $\{1, \dots, m+1\}$. Hence:

\Pr\left[ s(X_{n+1}, Y_{n+1}) \leq q_{1-\alpha} \right] \geq \frac{\lceil (m+1)(1-\alpha) \rceil}{m+1} \geq 1 - \alpha \tag{C.6}

$\square$

Corollary C.2 (Marginal coverage). The guarantee (C.5) is marginal: it holds on average over $X_{n+1}$. It does not guarantee conditional coverage given $X_{n+1} = x$.

C.3 Mondrian conformal prediction: conditional coverage

Definition C.2 (Mondrian conformal). Let $\{r(x)\}$ be a partition of the input space (e.g., workload regimes). For each regime $r$, define:

q_{1-\alpha}^{(r)} = \mathrm{Quantile}_{1-\alpha}\left( \{ s(X_i, Y_i) : i \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{cal}}, r(X_i) = r \} \cup \{+\infty\} \right) \tag{C.7}

The Mondrian prediction set is:

\mathcal{C}(x) = \left\{ y : s(x, y) \leq q_{1-\alpha}^{(r(x))} \right\} \tag{C.8}

Proposition C.1 (Conditional coverage). The Mondrian prediction set satisfies:

\Pr\left[ Y_{n+1} \in \mathcal{C}(X_{n+1}) \mid r(X_{n+1}) = r \right] \geq 1 - \alpha \tag{C.9}

Proof. Within each regime, the exchangeability of the calibration scores is preserved. Applying Theorem C.1 within each regime yields (C.9). $\square$

Remark C.1. Mondrian conformal is the appropriate tool for workload intervals because it guarantees coverage within each regime, in particular within spike regimes where robustness matters. The choice of regime partition $\{r\}$ is a modeling decision to be validated.

C.4 Copula-based joint uncertainty

Theorem C.2 (Sklar). Let $F$ be a joint cumulative distribution with marginals $F_W, F_\theta$. Then there exists a copula $C: [0,1]^2 \to [0,1]$ such that:

F(w, \theta) = C(F_W(w), F_\theta(\theta)) \tag{C.10}

If $F_W, F_\theta$ are continuous, $C$ is unique.

Proof. Standard; see Sklar (1959) and Nelsen (2006). $\square$

Definition C.3 (Copula-based joint uncertainty set). For a copula $C$ with dependence parameter $\vartheta$:

\mathcal{U}_{W\theta}(\vartheta) = \left\{ (w, \theta) : C(F_W(w), F_\theta(\theta); \vartheta) \leq 1 - \alpha \right\} \tag{C.11}

Conjecture C.1. For D2C systems, the dependence parameter $\vartheta$ is positive (upper-tail dependence), because workload spikes and parameter mis-estimation co-occur during transients. If confirmed, this implies that treating $\mathcal{U}_W$ and $\mathcal{U}_\theta$ independently underestimates the true robust feasibility set.

Proposition C.2 (Independence is a special case). With the independence copula $C_\perp(u, v) = uv$:

\mathcal{U}_{W\theta}(\vartheta = 0) = \mathcal{U}_W \times \mathcal{U}_\theta \tag{C.12}

Proof. Direct from (C.11) with $C(u, v) = uv$. $\square$

