Appendix D. Distributionally Robust Optimization

D.1 Wasserstein ambiguity set

Definition D.1 (Order-1 Wasserstein distance). For probability measures $\mathbb{P}, \mathbb{Q}$ on a metric space $(\Xi, d)$:

W_1(\mathbb{P}, \mathbb{Q}) = \inf_{\pi \in \Pi(\mathbb{P}, \mathbb{Q})} \int_{\Xi \times \Xi} d(\xi, \xi') \, d\pi(\xi, \xi') \tag{D.1}

where $\Pi(\mathbb{P}, \mathbb{Q})$ is the set of couplings with marginals $\mathbb{P}$ and $\mathbb{Q}$.

Definition D.2 (Wasserstein ambiguity set). Given an empirical distribution $\hat{\mathbb{P}}_t$ and radius $\rho_t$:

\mathcal{W}_t = \left\{ \mathbb{P} : W_1(\mathbb{P}, \hat{\mathbb{P}}_t) \leq \rho_t \right\} \tag{D.2}

D.2 Kantorovich-Rubinstein duality

Theorem D.1 (Kantorovich-Rubinstein). For any two probability measures:

W_1(\mathbb{P}, \mathbb{Q}) = \sup_{\|f\|_L \leq 1} \left( \mathbb{E}_\mathbb{P}[f] - \mathbb{E}_\mathbb{Q}[f] \right) \tag{D.3}

where the supremum is over 1-Lipschitz functions.

Proof. Standard; see Villani (2009). $\square$

Corollary D.1 (DRO reformulation). The distributionally robust problem

\min_u \max_{\mathbb{P} \in \mathcal{W}_t} \mathbb{E}_\mathbb{P}[J(u, w)] \tag{D.4}

is equivalent, under suitable regularity conditions, to:

\min_u \left\{ \mathbb{E}_{\hat{\mathbb{P}}_t}[J(u, w)] + \rho_t \cdot \mathrm{Lip}(J(u, \cdot)) \right\} \tag{D.5}

Proof. Apply Theorem D.1 to the inner supremum and exchange min/max under convexity. $\square$

D.3 Contextual DRO

Definition D.3. The CDRO problem is:

u^*_{t:t+H} = \arg\min_{u_{t:t+H}} \max_{\mathbb{P} \in \mathcal{P}(\rho(\zeta_t))} \mathbb{E}_\mathbb{P}\left[ \sum_{k=0}^{H} \left( \alpha E_{\mathrm{cool}} + \beta W_{\mathrm{net}} \right) \right] \tag{D.6}

where $\zeta_t$ is a real-time context vector and $\rho(\zeta_t)$ is a context-dependent radius.

Empirical anchor. Published CDRO results report approximately 13.7 percentage points reduction in the robustness cost premium relative to Min-Max MPC while maintaining near-zero thermal violations under spikes.

D.4 Adaptive radius: convergence properties

Definition D.4 (Adaptive rule). The Version 1 rule:

\rho_{t+1} = \rho_t + \kappa \left( |w_t - \hat{w}_t| - \rho_t \right) \tag{D.7}

Proposition D.1 (Boundedness, conditional). If the residual process $|w_t - \hat{w}_t|$ is bounded by $\bar{r}$, then $\rho_t$ remains in $[0, \max(\rho_0, \bar{r})]$ for all $t$ when $\kappa \in (0, 1)$.

Proof. The recursion $\rho_{t+1} = (1-\kappa)\rho_t + \kappa r_t$ is a convex combination of $\rho_t$ and $r_t$ when $\kappa \in (0,1)$. Hence $\rho_t$ is bounded by the running maximum of $\rho_0$ and the residuals. $\square$

Status. The convergence of $\rho_t$ to a fixed point of (D.7) is not established in general; this is a conjecture to be investigated. The CDRO variant replaces the hand-tuned adaptation with a context-dependent mapping $\rho(\zeta_t)$, which has been empirically validated but not theoretically derived.

