Appendix F. Feasibility and Pareto Theory

F.1 Pareto order: strict partial order

Definition F.1. $C_a \succ C_b$ iff $J_i(C_a) \leq J_i(C_b)$ for all $i$, with at least one strict inequality.

Proposition F.1. $\succ$ is a strict partial order.

Proof.
(i) Irreflexivity: $C_a \succ C_a$ would require a strict inequality $J_j(C_a) < J_j(C_a)$, impossible.
(ii) Asymmetry (implied by irreflexivity and transitivity): Suppose $C_a \succ C_b$ and $C_b \succ C_a$. Then $J(C_a) = J(C_b)$ componentwise, contradicting the strict inequality.
(iii) Transitivity: If $J(C_a) \leq J(C_b) \leq J(C_c)$ componentwise with at least one strict inequality at each stage, then $J(C_a) \leq J(C_c)$ componentwise with at least one strict inequality. $\square$

F.2 Feasibility set: conditional convexity

Proposition F.2. If $f$ is convex in $(u, w)$, the constraints $g_j \leq 0$ are convex in $(x, u, w)$, and $\mathcal{W}_t$ and $\mathcal{U}_\theta$ are convex, then $\mathcal{F}$ is convex.

Proof. For fixed $w, \theta$, the feasible set in $(x, u)$ is convex. Intersecting over $w \in \mathcal{W}_t$ and $\theta \in \mathcal{U}_\theta$ preserves convexity (arbitrary intersection of convex sets is convex). $\square$

Corollary F.1. If $\mathcal{F}$ is convex, it is connected.

Critical correction (retained). Without convexity assumptions, $\mathcal{F}$ can be disconnected. Nonlinear constraints (e.g., actuator rate limits, pressure drop bounds) can create disjoint feasibility regions. Connectivity cannot be decreed; it must be verified.

F.3 Performance set and boundary

Definition F.2. $\mathcal{R}_3 = \{ z \in \mathcal{F} : C_3 \text{ satisfies the performance criterion at } z \}$.

Definition F.3. $\Gamma_3 = \partial \mathcal{R}_3 = \overline{\mathcal{R}_3} \cap \overline{\mathcal{Z} \setminus \mathcal{R}_3}$.

Critical correction (retained). The Version 1 notation $\Gamma = \{z : C_3 \succ C_2\}$ conflated a set with its boundary. The correct distinction is between the set $\mathcal{R}_3$ and its boundary $\Gamma_3$.

Proposition F.3 (Boundary characterization). $z \in \Gamma_3$ iff every neighborhood of $z$ contains both points of $\mathcal{R}_3$ and points of $\mathcal{Z} \setminus \mathcal{R}_3$.

Proof. Standard definition of topological boundary. $\square$

