Appendix H. Stability of $\Gamma_3$

H.1 Probabilistic stability

Definition H.1. The justification boundary is probabilistically stable under $\mathcal{U}_\theta$ at level $\alpha$ if:

S_{\Gamma_3}(z) = \Pr_{\theta \sim \mathcal{U}_\theta^{\mathrm{stat}}} \left[ z \in \mathcal{R}_3(\theta) \right] \geq 1 - \alpha \tag{H.1}

Definition H.2 (Worst-case stability).

S_{\Gamma_3}^{WC}(z) = \inf_{\theta \in \mathcal{U}_\theta^{\mathrm{stat}}} \mathbb{1}_{\mathcal{R}_3(\theta)}(z) \tag{H.2}

Proposition H.1. $S_{\Gamma_3}^{WC}(z) \leq S_{\Gamma_3}(z)$.

Proof. The worst-case indicator is bounded above by the probability, since the probability is an average of indicators. $\square$

H.2 Hausdorff distance: metric properties

Definition H.3. For two sets $A, B$ in a metric space $(\mathcal{Z}, d)$:

d_H(A, B) = \max \left\{ \sup_{z \in A} \inf_{z' \in B} d(z, z'), \ \sup_{z' \in B} \inf_{z \in A} d(z, z') \right\} \tag{H.3}

Proposition H.2 (Metric properties). On the space of non-empty compact subsets of $\mathcal{Z}$, $d_H$ is a metric.

Proof. (i) Symmetry is immediate from the definition. (ii) $d_H(A, B) = 0$ iff $A = B$ for compact sets. (iii) Triangle inequality follows from the triangle inequality of $d$. $\square$

Critical correction (retained). For unbounded or empty sets, $d_H$ can be infinite or undefined. We restrict to a compact study region $\mathcal{Z}$ and use a truncated Hausdorff distance:

d_H^{\mathrm{trunc}}(A, B) = \min\left( d_H(A \cap \mathcal{Z}, B \cap \mathcal{Z}), \ D_{\max} \right) \tag{H.4}

H.3 R1–R4 partition: exhaustiveness

Proposition H.3. For any $z \in \mathcal{F}$, exactly one of R1–R4 holds.

Proof. Partition $\mathcal{U}_\theta^{\mathrm{stat}}$ into:

\mathcal{A}_+ = \{\theta : z \in \mathcal{R}_3(\theta)\}, \quad \mathcal{A}_- = \{\theta : z \notin \mathcal{R}_3(\theta)\} \tag{H.5}

The four regimes correspond to: (R1) $\mathcal{A}_- = \emptyset$; (R2) $\mathcal{A}_+ \neq \emptyset$, $\mathcal{A}_- \neq \emptyset$, $z \in \mathcal{R}_3(\hat\theta)$; (R3) $\mathcal{A}_+ \neq \emptyset$, $\mathcal{A}_- \neq \emptyset$, $z \notin \mathcal{R}_3(\hat\theta)$; (R4) $\mathcal{A}_+ = \emptyset$. These cases are mutually exclusive and exhaustive. $\square$

Operationalization. With $\alpha = 0.05$:

· R1 if $S_{\Gamma_3}(z) \geq 0.95$;
· R2 if $z \in \mathcal{R}_3(\hat\theta)$ and $0.5 \leq S_{\Gamma_3}(z) < 0.95$;
· R3 if $z \notin \mathcal{R}_3(\hat\theta)$ and $S_{\Gamma_3}(z) > 0.5$;
· R4 if $S_{\Gamma_3}(z) \leq 0.05$.

H.4 Lipschitz sensitivity bound

Proposition H.4. Let $z \in \mathcal{R}_3(\hat\theta)$ at distance $\varepsilon$ from $\partial \mathcal{R}_3(\hat\theta)$. If $\Gamma_3$ is locally $L_\Gamma$-Lipschitz in $\theta$:

d_H(\Gamma_3(\theta_1), \Gamma_3(\theta_2)) \leq L_\Gamma \|\theta_1 - \theta_2\| \tag{H.6}

then:

z \in \mathcal{R}_3(\theta) \quad \forall \theta \in \mathcal{U}_\theta^{\mathrm{stat}} \ \text{if} \ \|\theta - \hat\theta\| < \frac{\varepsilon}{L_\Gamma} \tag{H.7}

Proof. By Lipschitz continuity, the boundary displacement induced by a perturbation $\|\theta - \hat\theta\|$ is at most $L_\Gamma \|\theta - \hat\theta\| < \varepsilon$. Since $z$ is at distance $\varepsilon$ from the nominal boundary, it remains on the same side for all such perturbations. $\square$

Critical correction (retained). Lipschitz continuity is an assumption, not a theorem. It can fail near feasibility transitions, changes in the active MPC solution, regime switches, or numerical bifurcations.

H.5 Anisotropic sensitivity

Definition H.4. The Jacobian of the boundary with respect to parameters is:

J_\Gamma = \frac{\partial \Gamma_3}{\partial \theta} \tag{H.8}

Proposition H.5. The most sensitive direction in $\mathcal{Z}$ is the left singular vector of $J_\Gamma$ associated with the largest singular value.

Proof. By the SVD, the maximum amplification of a unit perturbation in $\theta$ is the largest singular value $\sigma_{\max}(J_\Gamma)$, achieved in the direction of the corresponding right singular vector. $\square$

Status. This is a candidate original contribution. The literature search did not find direct prior work on anisotropic boundary sensitivity for MPC justification.

H.6 Monte Carlo estimation of $S_{\Gamma_3}(z)$

Algorithm H.1.

1. Sample $\{\theta^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{M}$ from $\mathcal{U}_\theta^{\mathrm{stat}}$ (using the conformal or chi-square set).
2. For each $\theta^{(i)}$, determine whether $z \in \mathcal{R}_3(\theta^{(i)})$ by solving the robust feasibility problem.
3. Estimate $\hat{S}_{\Gamma_3}(z) = \frac{1}{M} \sum_{i=1}^{M} \mathbb{1}_{z \in \mathcal{R}_3(\theta^{(i)})}$.
4. Confidence interval via Clopper-Pearson.

Proposition H.6 (Convergence). $\hat{S}_{\Gamma_3}(z) \to S_{\Gamma_3}(z)$ almost surely as $M \to \infty$.

Proof. Strong law of large numbers applied to the i.i.d. indicators. $\square$

