Appendix I. Falsifiability Theory

I.1 Falsifiability theorem

Theorem I.1. A hypothesis $H$ is falsifiable iff there exists an observable outcome $O \in \mathcal{O}$ incompatible with $H$.

Proof. (⇒) If $H$ is falsifiable, by definition there exists a possible observation that would refute it. This is exactly the existence of $O \in \mathcal{O}$ incompatible with $H$. (⇐) If such $O$ exists, observing it refutes $H$; hence $H$ is falsifiable. $\square$

Corollary I.1. Falsifiability does not require that any kill criterion be satisfied. It requires that the criteria be definable and observable in principle.

I.2 Kill criteria: formal definitions

Definition I.1 (Kill F1). $\lambda_{\min}(\mathcal{I}(\theta)) < 10^{-6}$ for all admissible excitations.

Definition I.2 (Kill F2). $\kappa(\mathcal{I}) = \lambda_{\max}/\lambda_{\min} > 10^6$ under realistic noise.

Definition I.3 (Kill F3). $\lambda_{\min}(\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{band}}) < 10^{-4}$ for $\omega_H = 2\pi/(10\Delta t)$.

Definition I.4 (Kill F4). RMSE$(T_{\mathrm{model}} - T_{\mathrm{meas}}) > 1.0$°C on held-out data.

Definition I.5 (Kill F5). $d_H(\Gamma_3(\hat\theta), \Gamma_3(\theta)) > 0.1$ in normalized regime coordinates.

Definition I.6 (Kill F6). Pressure prediction error $> 20\%$.

Definition I.7 (Kill A). $\mathcal{R}_3^{\mathrm{nominal}} = \emptyset$.

Definition I.8 (Kill B). $|\mathcal{R}_3^{\mathrm{uncertain}}|/|\mathcal{R}_3^{\mathrm{nominal}}| < 0.5$.

Definition I.9 (Kill C). $C_4$ or $C_6$ Pareto-dominates $C_3$ on $> 70\%$ of sampled $z$.

I.3 Falsification protocol

Protocol I.1.

1. Declare all kill thresholds before any CONTROL run.
2. Execute the phases IDENTIFY, FORECAST, CONTROL, STRESS, MAP in order.
3. Evaluate each kill criterion at the end of the corresponding phase.
4. If any kill criterion is satisfied, the central hypothesis $H$ is refuted at that operating point.
5. The thesis reports the set of operating points where $H$ survives and the set where $H$ is refuted.

